E002 --> E005\n40\t    E003 --> E005\n41\t    E004 --> E005\n42\t```\n43\t\n44\t## DAG Justification\n45\t\n46\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n47\t|------|------|-----------|\n48\t| E001 → E002 | Rule 13 (API producer → consumer) | The Dashboard comparison view builds against the run-data retrieval capability the Data Science Pipelines backend provides; the consumer is serial after the producer. |\n49\t| E002 → E004 | Rule 12/13 (consumer builds against capability) | The registry entry point launches the comparison surface built by the Dashboard comparison epic; it cannot be built before that surface exists. |\n50\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (data/API producer → consumer) | The registry UI integration reads the run reference persisted on model versions by the Model Registry epic; it builds against that data extension. |\n51\t| E001 → E005 | Rule 11 (docs blocked by all Implementations) | Documentation describes the delivered comparison workflow. |\n52\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n53\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n54\t| E004 → E005 | Rule 11 | Same. |\n55\t\n56\tE001 and E003 are parallel (different components, different repos, no shared\n57\tartifacts — Rule 14).\n58\t\n59\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n60\t\n61\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) | Notes |\n62\t|-----|----------|---------|-------|\n63\t| HLR-1 | P0 | E001, E002 | Side-by-side comparison of parameters and metrics for selected runs. E001 provides the backend run-data support; E002 delivers the Dashboard user-facing capability. |\n64\t| HLR-2 | P1 | E003, E004 | Model-version-to-run linkage. Split across two components: E003 (Model Registry) persists the link; E004 (Dashboard) surfaces it and opens a comparison from the registry. |\n65\t| HLR-3 | P1 | E002 (bundled with HLR-1) | The diff view is a presentation mode of the same comparison surface over the same run data as HLR-1 (side-by-side parameters/metrics with changed values highlighted). HLR-1 and HLR-3 describe one capability consolidated from two customer councils — the strategy itself notes \"some overlap in wording was kept from the sources\". Covered by a single epic at the higher priority (P0); splitting would build the same surface twice plus an artificial serialization edge. |\n66\t| HLR-4 | P2 | E005 | Documentation of the comparison workflow from both entry points. |\n67\t\n68\tAll four HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements.\n69\t\n70\t## Health Warnings\n71\t\n72\t- **Missing strategy sections**: the strategy provides no Impacted Teams table, no per-component \"what changes\" detail, no Dependencies table, no Given/When/Then acceptance criteria, and no NFRs. Owner teams were derived from the architecture-context files (`.context/architecture-context/architecture/rhoai-3.0/`). Epic acceptance criteria below were derived from the HLRs at a functional level.\n73\t- **Ambiguous role of Data Science Pipelines**: the strategy lists Data Science Pipelines as affected but does not state what changes. It is treated as an active component (E001: run-data retrieval support for multi-run comparison) because pipeline-run parameters and metrics live in the DSP backend that the Dashboard consumes (per `data-science-pipelines.md`). If per-run APIs already in place prove sufficient for comparison retrieval, E001 collapses to validation acceptance criteria on the Dashboard comparison epic and the DAG shortens.\n74\t- **\"Training runs\" provenance unspecified**: the strategy compares \"training and pipeline runs\" but the affected components do not include the Training Operator or a metadata store beyond Data Science Pipelines. E001's scope is written at the functional level (run kinds surfaced in the platform's runs list) to avoid inventing a training-run data source; the implementing teams resolve where training-run parameters and metrics are read from.\n75\t\n76\t## Tiered Delivery\n77\t\n78\tNot applicable — no external dependencies with uncertain timing; all work is\n79\tin-platform.\n80\t"}]},"parent_tool_